C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      HW3 - Reservoir Operation Problem
       
      THE PROBLEM:
      A reservoir is designed to provide water for irrigation.  The reservoir has a capacity of 9,000 acre-feet.
      Initial storage is 3,000 acre-feet of water. The size of the diversion canal and farmed area is very large
      relative to the amount of irrigation water available, so there is no upper limit on usable irrigation water.
      Any water above the reservoir capacity must be released to the river (spill). The ending storage must be equal
      to or greater than the begin¬ning storage.  The benefits per unit of water, and the estimated inflows to the
      reservoir in 3 months are given in Table 1. What is the diversion schedule that maximizes benefits?
       
      Data:
      Month     Inflow (ac-ft)    Benefit ($/ac-ft)
      June      5,000             150
      July      3,200             170
      August    2,000             425
       
      Initial Storage: 3,000 ac-ft
      Max Storage:     9,000 ac-ft
      Ending Storage:  >= 3,000 ac-ft
       
      Madison Wilcox
      9/29/26
  26   
  27  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  28  SETS t Time periods (months) / June, July, August /;
  29   
  30  * 2. DEFINE input data
  31  SCALARS
  32     S_init Initial reservoir storage at start of June (ac-ft) / 3000 /
  33     S_max  Maximum reservoir storage capacity (ac-ft)         / 9000 /;
  34   
  35  PARAMETERS
  36     Inflow(t) Forecasted monthly inflows (ac-ft)
  37           / June    5000,
  38             July    3200,
  39             August  2000 /
  40   
  41     Benefit(t) Economic benefit per unit of diverted water ($ per ac-ft)
  42           / June    150,
  43             July    170,
  44             August  425 /;
  45   
  46  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  47  VARIABLES
  48     D(t)
  49     S(t)
  50     Spill(t)
  51     VPROFIT  ;
  52   
  53  * Non-negativity constraints
  54  POSITIVE VARIABLES D, S, Spill;
  55   
  56  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  57  EQUATIONS
  58     PROFIT
  59     MassBalance(t)
  60     CapBound(t)
  61     EndStorage     ;
  62   
  63  * Objective Function
  64  PROFIT..
  65     VPROFIT =E= SUM(t, Benefit(t) * D(t));
  66   
  67  * Mass Balance Equation: Storage_t = Storage_t-1 + Inflow_t - Diversion_t - Spill_t
  68  MassBalance(t)..
  69     S(t) =E= (S_init$SAMEAS(t,'June') + S(t-1)$(NOT SAMEAS(t,'June')))
  70              + Inflow(t) - D(t) - Spill(t);
  71   
  72  * Reservoir Capacity Constraint
  73  CapBound(t)..
  74     S(t) =L= S_max;
  75   
  76  * Ending Storage Constraint: August ending storage >= Initial June storage
  77  EndStorage..
  78     S('August') =G= S_init;
  79   
  80  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  81  MODEL ReservoirOps / ALL /;
  82   
  83  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
  84  SOLVE ReservoirOps USING LP MAXIMIZING VPROFIT;
  85   
  86  * 7. DISPLAY RESULTS
  87  DISPLAY D.L, S.L, Spill.L, VPROFIT.L, MassBalance.M, CapBound.M, EndStorage.M;


